  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They never trailed and didn't let the early lead slip.",
    "what_this_means": "Rod\u00f3n lasted five innings with seven hits allowed but nothing catastrophic, and that proved enough because the offense did something simpler: it scored early and often. Two runs in the first. Two more in the second. Then five in the fifth. Bellinger drove in two of them with a home run, Goldschmidt added another. And the game stopped being a game sometime around the time the score hit 10-3. This is what balance looks like when both sides click at once. The pitching didn't dominate, but it didn't break. The offense wasn't quiet, and when it mattered most, it spoke loudly. Blackburn and Headrick bridged into the late innings without incident. There was no moment where the margin felt threatened. Four straight wins now, and this one felt inevitable from inning two onward.",
    "what_to_watch": "The division lead is real, and so is the window to build it wider. These are the games you win when everything cooperates. Rotation gives you innings, the lineup strings together hits with runners in position, the bullpen doesn't have to be heroic because the game is already decided. Won't always be this clean, but this is the template.",
